export const SAMPLE_NOTES: ReadonlyArray<{ domain: string; text: string }> = [
  { domain: "Business", text: "A technique for producing ideas\n\nJames Webb Young, an advertising executive, wrote a tiny book in 1940 called A Technique for Producing Ideas. You can read it in an hour. People in advertising still pass it around.\n\nHis core claim: an idea is nothing more or less than a new combination of old elements. And the ability to make new combinations depends mostly on your ability to see relationships between things.\n\nHis five steps:\n1. Gather raw material. Both the specific stuff about your problem and lots of general stuff about life and everything else.\n2. Work it over. Look at the pieces together, try to fit them, feel stuck.\n3. Drop it. Go do something else completely. Listen to music, go to the movies. Let the unconscious do its thing.\n4. The idea shows up, usually when you're not looking for it. Shaving, in the bath, waking up.\n5. Shape it. Take it out into the real world and let it get criticized and improved.\n\nHe says most people skip step 1 for general knowledge, so they have nothing to combine. Everything you've read is a potential piece of a future idea.\n\nSame pattern as Poincaré. Work, let go, flash." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Amazon banned PowerPoint\n\nIn 2004, Jeff Bezos stopped PowerPoint presentations in Amazon's senior leadership meetings. Instead, whoever's presenting writes a memo, up to six pages, in full sentences.\n\nMeetings start with everyone reading the memo in silence, for 20 or 30 minutes. Then they discuss.\n\nWhy he did it, from his own explanation: bullet points let you hide sloppy thinking. You can skip the connections between ideas. Writing full paragraphs forces you to understand which ideas matter more and how they relate.\n\nThe silent reading part matters too. Everyone actually reads it (no pretending you did at home). And people who are quieter get the same information as the loud ones before the arguing starts.\n\nSide effect: meetings got shorter and more decisive, because the thinking was already done on paper.\n\nI tried this once with a decision at work. Writing it out took me two hours and I changed my own mind halfway through." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Bezos and the regret minimization framework\n\n1994. Jeff Bezos is 30, doing well at a hedge fund in New York, and wants to quit to start an online bookstore. His boss takes him on a long walk in Central Park and says it's a good idea, but a better idea for someone who doesn't already have a good job. Think about it for 48 hours.\n\nHow Bezos decided, by his own account: he imagined himself at 80, looking back on his life, and asked what he'd regret. He knew he wouldn't regret trying and failing at the internet. He'd regret never trying. A missed bonus wouldn't matter at 80.\n\nSo he left, drove across the country with his wife while writing the business plan, and started Amazon in a garage.\n\nWhat I like as a tool: it gets you out of the short term noise. Most decisions feel huge because the cost is right here (a salary, an awkward conversation) and the benefit is far away. Projecting to 80 flips that.\n\nWhat I'm wary of: it's a great story because it worked. Plenty of people quit on the same logic and it didn't." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Brooks's law\n\nFred Brooks managed IBM's OS/360 project in the 1960s, one of the biggest software projects of its time. It went badly late. He wrote about what he learned in The Mythical Man-Month (1975), which programmers still quote.\n\nThe famous line: adding people to a late software project makes it later.\n\nWhy:\n- New people need time to get up to speed, and the people who train them are the ones who were doing the work.\n- Some tasks can't be split. His example: nine women can't make a baby in one month.\n- Every person you add increases the number of people who need to talk to each other. Communication paths grow much faster than headcount. 5 people have 10 pairs. 10 people have 45.\n\nThe title is the joke. A \"man-month\" assumes people and months are interchangeable. They're not.\n\nApplies way outside software. Any project where the work depends on shared understanding that lives in people's heads." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Ford got the assembly line from a slaughterhouse\n\nThe moving assembly line at Ford's Highland Park plant started in 1913, and it changed manufacturing. Building a Model T chassis went from around twelve hours to about an hour and a half.\n\nThe idea didn't come from car people. Chicago meatpackers had been running \"disassembly lines\" for years. Carcasses hung from overhead trolleys and moved past workers, each of whom made the same cut over and over. One Ford engineer, William Klann, had seen this in Chicago and brought the idea back.\n\nFord ran it in reverse. Instead of taking an animal apart piece by piece, put a car together piece by piece. The worker stays, the work moves.\n\nFord himself said he didn't invent anything new, he just put together the discoveries of other people.\n\nCopying from your own industry makes you a little better. Copying from a completely different industry is where the big jumps come from." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Is my book idea original enough?\n\nAn editor from a small press read my newsletter and asked if I'd ever thought about a book. I have. I've been thinking about it for two years. Now I have to write a proposal and every time I try I freeze on the same question: is this even new?\n\nEverything in it comes from somewhere else. A bit from my research, a framework from a business book, examples from history, a lot of ideas I've written about in the newsletter already. When I list my sources it looks like I'm just remixing other people.\n\nI keep reading \"comparable titles\" for the proposal and every one makes me feel like it's been done.\n\nWhat I can't figure out:\n- what makes a combination of old ideas actually new?\n- how do I explain the angle so it doesn't sound like a summary of other books?\n\nProposal due August 1." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Jobs took a calligraphy class\n\nFrom Steve Jobs's Stanford commencement speech, 2005.\n\nHe dropped out of Reed College after one semester, but kept hanging around and sitting in on classes he found interesting. One was calligraphy. He learned about serif and sans serif typefaces, the spacing between letters, what makes good typography beautiful. No practical use for any of it in his life, he said.\n\nTen years later, designing the first Macintosh, it all came back. The Mac was the first computer with beautiful typography, multiple typefaces and proportional spacing. He said if he had never dropped in on that one course, the Mac would never have had them.\n\nHis point was that you can't connect the dots looking forward, only looking backward. You have to trust they'll connect somehow.\n\nMy only problem with this: the dots connected because Jobs happened to remember the class. How many dots do the rest of us have that never connect, just because we never think of them at the right moment?" },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Kodak invented the digital camera\n\n1975. Steve Sasson, a young engineer at Kodak, builds the first digital camera. It's the size of a toaster, takes about 23 seconds to record one black and white picture at 0.01 megapixels onto a cassette tape, and needs a special setup to view it on a TV.\n\nHe shows it to management. As he tells the story, the reaction was roughly: that's cute, but don't tell anyone about it. Kodak made its money on film and printing. Why would anyone want to look at photos on a TV?\n\nKodak did keep working on digital and even held key patents. But the business never moved. It had the future in its own lab for decades. Kodak filed for bankruptcy in 2012.\n\nThe usual lesson is \"innovate or die.\" The lesson I take is narrower. Kodak didn't forget the camera. It remembered it fine. What it couldn't do was let that old idea change what it was doing right now.\n\nRemembering something and letting it change the work are different things." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Maker's schedule, manager's schedule\n\nPaul Graham essay from 2009. Short and it changed how I look at my calendar.\n\nTwo kinds of schedules:\n- Manager's schedule: the day is cut into one hour blocks. You change what you're doing every hour. A meeting is just one more block.\n- Maker's schedule: programmers, writers, designers need long uninterrupted chunks, at least half a day. You can't write well or build anything hard in one hour.\n\nFor a maker, a single meeting in the middle of the afternoon doesn't cost an hour. It can blow up the whole afternoon, because there's not enough time on either side to get into deep work.\n\nHis solution at Y Combinator: keep meetings at the end of the day, or cluster them. Protect the big blocks.\n\nThe problem is managers schedule meetings without seeing what they cost the people who have maker schedules. It looks like one hour on the calendar." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Phil Knight kept his day job\n\nThe founding of Nike is usually told like a bold leap. It wasn't. Phil Knight started importing Japanese running shoes in 1964, selling them out of the trunk of his car at track meets. For years he kept working as an accountant, including at Price Waterhouse, and taught accounting at Portland State University. The shoe company was the side thing until it could pay for itself. He went full time around 1969.\n\nSame pattern with Sara Blakely. She kept her job selling fax machines door to door while she developed Spanx, for about two years. She wrote the patent herself to save money and only quit when the product was going to stores.\n\nThere's a study on this too, from 2014 (Raffiee and Feng). Founders who kept their day jobs while starting a business were substantially less likely to fail than those who quit to go all in.\n\nThe movies tell you to burn the boats. A lot of the real stories are people who built a lifeboat first.\n\nNot saying it's always right. Just that \"quit and jump\" is not the only heroic way to do it." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Pixar's Braintrust\n\nFrom Ed Catmull, Creativity, Inc. (2014). Catmull co-founded Pixar.\n\nHis starting point: early on, all of Pixar's movies are bad. He calls them ugly babies. Toy Story 2 was so bad deep into production that they rebuilt most of it in a very short time.\n\nThe Braintrust is how they fix them. A group of experienced directors and storytellers watch the movie in progress every few months and give totally honest notes.\n\nRules that make it work:\n- Notes are about the movie, not the person.\n- The Braintrust has no authority. The director decides what to do with the notes. Taking away the power to force changes is what lets people be candid.\n- They point out problems, they don't dictate solutions.\n\nWhat I found striking: they treat candor as something you have to design for. It doesn't happen on its own, especially once there's a hierarchy. People tell the boss what the boss wants to hear.\n\nSmall group, regular, honest, no power. Closer to a pit crew review than a status meeting." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Post-it notes were a failed glue\n\n1968. Spencer Silver, a chemist at 3M, is trying to make a super strong adhesive. He makes the opposite. A glue that sticks, but lightly, and peels off clean without leaving residue. It can be reused.\n\nHe thinks it's interesting, but nobody can think of what it's for. For years he goes around 3M presenting it in seminars. People called it a solution looking for a problem.\n\n1974. Art Fry, another 3M scientist, sings in his church choir. His paper bookmarks keep falling out of his hymnal. Annoying, every Sunday. He remembers Silver's glue from one of those seminars and thinks: a bookmark that sticks but doesn't tear the page.\n\nThen they figured out the bigger use: little notes you can stick anywhere. After some false starts, Post-it Notes launched nationally in 1980 and became one of the most used office products in the world.\n\nSix years between the glue and the use. It needed a person carrying the old idea to bump into the right annoyance. Fry happened to have been in the right seminar and happened to remember it on a Sunday." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Should I leave academia?\n\nThe question I keep circling. The newsletter has 3,000 subscribers now. The book proposal went out. A company reached out about a research job that pays almost double my stipend.\n\nAnd the defense is eight months away. Five years of work.\n\nThe voice in my head: you're not going to get a tenure track job anyway, the market is brutal, why spend another year on a credential? Every month you wait is a month you could be building the writing career for real.\n\nThe other voice: you're so close. People who leave in the last year regret it. And 3,000 newsletter subscribers isn't a living.\n\nSome days I'm writing the email to my advisor. Some days I feel embarrassed for even considering it.\n\nPart of me just wants someone to tell me to go." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "The group paper is late\n\nFive authors, one paper, a journal deadline in six weeks. We're behind. Sections are written by different people and the handoffs are a mess: someone updates the data and the results section doesn't change, someone changes a term in the intro and nobody else uses it, two people rewrote the same paragraph last week without knowing.\n\nThe PI's solution today: bring in two more grad students to \"help push it over the line.\"\n\nSomething about that feels wrong but I couldn't say why in the meeting. The hard part is the analysis section, and only Ana and I really understand what we did there. We already spend half our time explaining it to the others.\n\nI have to send the PI a plan by Friday. Either I make the \"more people\" plan work, or I push back with something real. Also we have a 90 minute \"sync\" every Tuesday that mostly involves reading status updates out loud." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "Toyota learned from an American supermarket\n\nTaiichi Ohno, the engineer behind the Toyota Production System, had a problem in the 1950s. Factories made huge piles of parts \"just in case,\" and those piles cost money, took space and hid mistakes.\n\nHe'd heard about how American supermarkets worked, and later saw them himself. The store doesn't stock months of milk. A customer takes a carton, the shelf shows a gap, and the store refills exactly what was taken. The customer pulls, the store responds.\n\nOhno flipped the factory to work the same way. Each station only makes what the next station actually took. To signal it, they used cards, kanban, that travel with the parts. Empty bin plus a card means \"make more of this.\" No card, no production.\n\nThis became just-in-time manufacturing, and half the world copied it from Toyota.\n\nThe best part: he got the idea for a car factory from people buying groceries. He saw a pattern in a completely ordinary place and recognized it as the answer to his problem." },
  { domain: "Business", text: "What a hospital learned from Ferrari's pit crew\n\nGreat Ormond Street Hospital in London, early 2000s. Two heart surgeons there, Martin Elliott and Allan Goldman, have a problem with the handover. After a child's heart surgery, the patient is moved from the operating room to intensive care, with all the machines, lines and information that go with them. It was messy. Too many people, nobody clearly in charge, information falling through the cracks.\n\nAfter a long shift they were watching Formula 1 on TV and saw a pit stop. Around 20 people changing tires and refueling a car in seconds, without a word.\n\nThey thought: that's our handover, but done right.\n\nSo they went and studied it with the Ferrari and McLaren teams. What they took back:\n- one person clearly in charge (in F1, the lollipop man)\n- everyone has exactly one job and knows it\n- the sequence is fixed and practiced, the same every time\n- a short checklist, and nobody talks unless it's needed\n- they rehearse\n\nThey redesigned the handover around this. Their published study found technical mistakes and missing information dropped noticeably.\n\nThe answer to a medical problem was on Sunday afternoon TV." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Apollo 13: square peg, round hole\n\nApril 1970. An oxygen tank explodes on the way to the Moon. The three astronauts move into the lunar module, which was designed for two people for about two days, to use as a lifeboat.\n\nNew problem: carbon dioxide is building up. The lunar module's filters can't keep up. There are spare filters from the command module, but they're square, and the lunar module's sockets are round.\n\nEngineers in Houston dump on a table exactly what the astronauts have on board. Plastic bags, cardboard covers from the flight manuals, a sock, hoses from the spacesuits, and duct tape. They have to figure out how to make the square filter work in the round system using only that, then explain it to the crew over the radio.\n\nThey do it. The astronauts build the adapter, the CO2 levels drop, and they get home.\n\nWhat I take from it: the answer was already on board. They didn't need anything new. They needed someone to look at what they already had and see it differently, under pressure, fast." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Archimedes in the bath\n\nThe story, as the Roman writer Vitruvius told it about two hundred years later. King Hiero of Syracuse had a gold crown made and suspected the goldsmith had mixed in cheaper silver. He asked Archimedes to find out without damaging the crown.\n\nArchimedes was stuck. Then he got into a full bath, watched the water rise as his body went in, and realized the answer: an object pushes aside its own volume of water. Silver is lighter than gold, so a crown with silver in it would be bulkier and push aside more water than the same weight of pure gold. He was so excited he jumped out and ran home naked through the streets shouting \"Eureka!\" (I've found it).\n\nHistorians doubt it happened like that. But the reason everyone remembers it is that it feels true. The answer comes when you've stopped staring at the problem, while you're doing something ordinary, because the problem was loaded into your head and something in the world matched it.\n\nThe crown didn't solve itself. The bath did, because he'd already done the hard thinking." },
  { domain: "History", text: "As We May Think\n\nVannevar Bush, July 1945, in The Atlantic. Bush had run the US government's scientific research during the war. As the war ended, he wrote about what scientists should work on next.\n\nHis worry: humans were producing knowledge faster than anyone could use it. Important findings got lost in the pile. (He actually brings up Mendel, whose work was lost for a generation because the right people didn't see it.)\n\nHis idea was a desk he called the memex. It would store all your books, records and notes on microfilm, and let you call them up quickly. The key part wasn't the storage, it was what he called associative trails. He pointed out that the mind doesn't work like an index, alphabetically or by subject. It jumps from one item to the next by association, the way one thought suggests another. He wanted a machine that followed those connections.\n\nPeople credit this essay as an early inspiration for hypertext and the web.\n\n80 years later we have the storage, way more than he imagined. What we still mostly don't have is the second half: the thing that makes the right item come back on its own, at the moment you need it." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Darwin read Malthus for fun\n\nSeptember 1838. Darwin is 29, back from the Beagle, already convinced species change over time but missing the mechanism. He picks up Thomas Malthus's Essay on the Principle of Population. Not for work. By his own account, for amusement.\n\nMalthus is writing about people and economics. Populations grow faster than the food supply, so there's always a struggle, and most don't make it.\n\nDarwin reads that and it clicks. If more creatures are born than can survive, then any small advantage decides who lives. Good variations get kept, bad ones get lost. He had his theory.\n\nAn economics book about human poverty handed biology its biggest idea. He wasn't looking for it there. He couldn't have found it with a search, because he didn't know the answer lived in economics.\n\nThen he sat on it for 20 years." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Edison's 3,500 notebooks\n\nEdison and his teams left behind roughly 3,500 notebooks. Millions of pages. Every experiment, failure, sketch, cost, half idea.\n\nFor the lightbulb filament they tried thousands of materials. Platinum, cotton thread, paper, fishing line, supposedly even hair from a staff member's beard. Eventually carbonized bamboo, which could burn for over a thousand hours.\n\nThe famous line about finding thousands of ways that don't work (probably not his exact words, but the idea is right) only works if you remember the thousands. The notebooks were how the lab avoided testing the same dead end twice. They were memory for a whole team.\n\nWhat I keep thinking: even that system depended on someone remembering to go back and look. A notebook doesn't tap you on the shoulder. Edison could pay a lab full of assistants who knew where things were. Most of us are our own assistant, and we're bad at the job." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Gutenberg borrowed the wine press\n\nJohannes Gutenberg, Mainz, around 1450. Printing with carved wooden blocks already existed, and in East Asia movable type had been made centuries earlier. What Gutenberg put together for Europe was a system: metal type that could be cast quickly in a mold, an oil based ink that would stick to metal, and a press.\n\nThe press was the part he didn't invent. Screw presses had been used for a long time in the region for squeezing grapes and olives, and for pressing paper and cloth. He adapted it to push paper evenly onto inked type.\n\nHis background helped. He'd worked with metal, and at one point made small metal mirrors for pilgrims. So he combined metalworking, a winemaking tool and a goldsmith's knowledge of alloys.\n\nThe thing that changed history was a collage of things that already existed, from places that had nothing to do with books." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Leonardo: hair moves like water\n\nOne of my favorite pages. Leonardo is studying how water moves, how it pours, splits, curls back into itself. And he writes that hair does the same thing. Watch the surface of water, he says (paraphrasing), and see how it's like hair, which has two motions, one from its weight and one from the direction of its curl.\n\nSo his water drawings look like hair, and his angels' curls look like whirlpools. Same study.\n\nThis is what cross-domain thinking actually looks like up close. Not a grand theory. Just noticing that the thing you're drawing today behaves like the thing you drew last spring, and letting the old drawing teach the new one.\n\nHe could do it because both were in the same notebook, sometimes on the same page. He was his own search engine. That only works while you can hold the whole notebook in your head, and past a certain point not even Leonardo could." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Leonardo: \"tell me if anything was ever done\"\n\nLeonardo da Vinci left around 7,000 pages of notes that survive. Probably a lot more got lost. Flying machines, canal locks, anatomy, a mechanical knight, how light falls on a face, shopping lists, money he lent his assistant Salaì, who kept stealing from him.\n\nScattered through those pages, across different years, he tests his pen by writing the same line: dimmi se mai fu fatta cosa alcuna. Tell me if anything was ever done.\n\nHe had reasons to ask. Things he started and didn't finish:\n- The Sforza horse. Meant to be the biggest bronze horse in the world. He made a full size clay model in 1493. The bronze set aside for it was sent off to make cannons in 1494. French soldiers later used the clay horse for target practice.\n- The Adoration of the Magi, left unfinished when he moved to Milan.\n- The Battle of Anghiari, a huge mural that started running because he experimented with the paint.\n- Treatises on painting, on water, on anatomy. None published while he lived.\n\nThe anatomy one hurts most. His drawings of the heart were centuries ahead. Some of what he worked out about how heart valves close wasn't confirmed until modern imaging. Nobody could use it, because nobody could read it. It was in private notebooks, in mirror writing, in somebody's house.\n\nThe ideas were never the problem. The ideas were there. What he didn't have was a way for the right page to come back at the right moment, to him or to anyone else." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Newton couldn't find his own proof\n\nAugust 1684. Edmond Halley rides up to Cambridge to ask Isaac Newton a question that has been bugging him, Wren and Hooke for months: if the sun pulls on the planets with a force that weakens with the square of the distance, what shape would their orbits be?\n\nNewton answers right away. An ellipse. He'd already calculated it.\n\nHalley, thrilled, asks to see the calculation. Newton goes through his papers and can't find it.\n\nThink about that. The man who would write the most important science book in history had already solved its central problem, and he lost the paper. He promised to redo it and send it along.\n\nHe did. In November a short treatise arrived, De motu corporum in gyrum. Halley pushed him to keep going. Three years later, the Principia.\n\nTwo things I keep thinking about:\n1. If Halley hadn't shown up with the right question at the right time, that proof might have stayed lost in a pile at Trinity. Somebody had to trigger the retrieval.\n2. Losing the paper wasn't the disaster. Redoing it with fresh eyes made it bigger. The old work mattered because it got pulled back into a new moment.\n\nGenius doesn't protect you from your own filing." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Paul McCartney dreamed \"Yesterday\"\n\nMid 1960s. Paul McCartney wakes up in London with a complete melody in his head. He goes to the piano and plays it. It's beautiful, and that's exactly what worries him. It came too easily. He's convinced he must have heard it somewhere and is just remembering someone else's song.\n\nSo for weeks he plays it for people. Other musicians, friends, music industry people. Have you heard this before? Whose is it? Nobody knows it. While he's checking, he uses a joke working title, \"Scrambled Eggs,\" as placeholder words.\n\nEventually he accepts it's his. \"Yesterday\" became one of the most recorded songs in history.\n\nHe was right to worry, though. There's a name for it: cryptomnesia, when a forgotten memory comes back and feels like a brand new idea. George Harrison lost a lawsuit in 1976 because a court found his song \"My Sweet Lord\" had subconsciously copied an older hit, \"He's So Fine.\" The judge said he didn't do it on purpose. He'd just forgotten he'd heard it.\n\nSo your brain does surface old material on its own. It just strips off the label saying where it came from." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Ramanujan's lost notebook\n\nSrinivasa Ramanujan, mostly self-taught, sends a letter packed with formulas to G.H. Hardy at Cambridge in 1913. Hardy figures it's either a fraud or a genius, and decides a fraud would have to be even more brilliant. Ramanujan comes to England, produces astonishing results, gets sick, goes home to India and dies in 1920. He was 32.\n\nIn his last year, very ill, he kept writing. Those pages went to England, passed between people, and ended up among the papers of a mathematician named Watson, which went to the library at Trinity College after Watson died.\n\nSpring 1976. George Andrews, an American mathematician visiting Trinity, goes through those boxes and finds around a hundred pages of Ramanujan's last work. It had been sitting in a library for more than fifty years.\n\nMathematicians have been working through it for decades since. Some of the ideas later turned up in physics, including work on black holes, which Ramanujan had no way of knowing.\n\nThe detail I love: it wasn't destroyed. The library had it. It just wasn't findable by anyone who didn't already know to look." },
  { domain: "History", text: "The O-ring memo\n\nIn July 1985, an engineer at Morton Thiokol named Roger Boisjoly wrote a memo about the rubber O-rings that sealed the joints of the space shuttle's solid rocket boosters. He'd seen damage on boosters recovered from earlier flights, worse when it had been cold. His memo warned that if this wasn't fixed, the result could be catastrophic.\n\nJanuary 27, 1986, the night before the Challenger launch. Forecast for Florida: well below freezing overnight, much colder than any previous launch. Boisjoly and other engineers argued against launching. There was a teleconference. Under pressure, managers overrode them.\n\nThe next morning Challenger broke apart 73 seconds after launch. All seven crew members died. The investigation found the cause was the O-ring seal failing in the cold.\n\nThe warning existed. It was on paper, months earlier, with photos. The knowledge was in the building. It lost to the schedule.\n\nI keep this note because it's the clearest case I know of an organization that had the answer and didn't use it at the one moment it mattered." },
  { domain: "History", text: "The Wright brothers twisted a box\n\nEveryone was trying to build planes with more power. The Wrights realized the real problem was control. How do you keep the thing balanced and turn it?\n\nThey watched birds, and noticed buzzards tilt the tips of their wings differently to roll and balance. Fine, but how do you do that with a wooden wing?\n\nSummer 1899, in their bicycle shop in Dayton. Wilbur is talking to a customer while holding an empty cardboard box that had held a bicycle inner tube. He absentmindedly twists the box, and sees the top surface twist, one end up and one end down, while the box stays solid.\n\nThat's it. Twist the whole wing. They called it wing warping, and it's the ancestor of how planes still turn.\n\nThe bicycle business also mattered more than people think. They already understood balance (riding a bike is constant small corrections), light strong frames, and chains and sprockets for the propellers.\n\nBirds for the idea, a cardboard box for the mechanism, bicycles for the know how. None of that was \"aviation.\"" },
  { domain: "History", text: "The man who cut Leonardo's notebooks apart\n\nAfter Leonardo died in 1519, his notebooks went to his student Francesco Melzi, who looked after them for fifty years. When Melzi died, his son treated them like old paper. Pages walked out of the house.\n\nThen a sculptor named Pompeo Leoni collected as many as he could and did something that sounds responsible. He organized them. He cut pages apart and pasted them into big albums, roughly by subject. Machines and technical drawings went into one enormous volume (now the Codex Atlanticus in Milan). A lot of the anatomy and nature drawings ended up in another collection (now at Windsor Castle).\n\nEngineering here. Art and anatomy over there. Tidy.\n\nThe problem: Leonardo didn't think in categories. A page might have water swirling around a post right next to curls of hair, because to him they were the same problem. A gear next to a muscle. Leoni's scissors cut those neighbors apart. Scholars have spent centuries trying to work out which pages originally sat next to which.\n\nI think about this every time I make a new folder.\n\nSorting by topic is how you find what you're looking for. It's also how you make sure you'll never stumble onto the connection between two things you'd never think to put together. Part of the value of Leonardo's pages was what was sitting next to what." },
  { domain: "History", text: "The plane that was too complicated to fly\n\nOctober 30, 1935. Boeing's Model 299, the prototype that became the B-17 bomber, takes off for a demo for the Army Air Corps at Wright Field. It climbs, stalls, and crashes. Two of the crew die, including the pilot, Major Ployer Hill, one of the Army's most experienced test pilots.\n\nThe cause: he'd forgotten to release a lock on the elevator and rudder controls. A simple step, missed.\n\nThe press said it was too much plane for one man to fly. Way more steps than earlier planes.\n\nThe pilots didn't decide to train harder. The fix was almost embarrassingly simple. A checklist. One card for takeoff, flight, before landing, after landing. Things any pilot already knew.\n\nWith that checklist, B-17s went on to fly huge distances without an accident during testing, and the Army ordered thousands.\n\nThe lesson I keep getting from this: the problem wasn't knowledge. Hill knew about the lock. Knowing something and remembering it at the exact moment you need it are two different skills, and experts are not exempt from the second one." },
  { domain: "History", text: "Wallace remembered Malthus with a fever\n\n1858, the Malay Archipelago. Alfred Russel Wallace is laid up with malaria. Shaking, feverish, nothing to do but think.\n\nHe'd been wondering for years why some animals survive and others don't. And lying there, something he'd read about twelve years earlier came back: Malthus, on population and the things that keep it in check.\n\nThe old reading showed up at the right moment and, as he remembered it, he had the idea of natural selection worked out within a couple of hours. He wrote it up and mailed it to... Charles Darwin.\n\nDarwin got the letter in June 1858 and saw that someone had reached his idea independently. That finally pushed him to publish. On the Origin of Species came out the next year.\n\nSame old book, two different men, years apart, both retrieving it exactly when their own problem was ripe.\n\nNote to self: the reading wasn't wasted just because it took twelve years to pay off. It needed the right question to come along." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Atomic Habits notes\n\nJames Clear, Atomic Habits. Read January 2025, like everyone else in January.\n\nThe ideas I actually want to remember:\n\nTwo minute rule. When starting a new habit, shrink it until it takes two minutes. \"Read before bed\" becomes \"read one page.\" \"Go for a run\" becomes \"put on running shoes.\" The point is to master showing up before you try to improve. A habit has to exist before it can be optimized.\n\nIdentity. Don't aim for \"run a marathon,\" aim for \"I'm someone who runs.\" Every time you do it, even small, it's a vote for that identity.\n\nNever miss twice. Missing once is an accident. Missing twice is the start of a new habit.\n\nMake it easy, make it satisfying. If the habit feels bad every time, you will stop. Willpower doesn't beat a habit that feels like punishment.\n\nEnvironment over motivation. Put the thing where you'll see it. Make the bad option harder to reach.\n\nThe British cycling story is in here too. Dave Brailsford's team improved hundreds of tiny things by about 1% each (better pillows, washing hands to avoid colds, heated shorts) and the gains added up." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Bread class: watch the dough, not the clock\n\nNotes from the Saturday bread class at the community kitchen, spring 2025. The teacher was an old baker who'd run a bakery for 30 years. Grumpy, great.\n\nWhat I wrote down:\n- Fermentation runs on temperature, not time. Recipe times assume a warm kitchen, somewhere around 24-26C (75-78F). Every few degrees colder can make fermentation take much longer.\n- So a recipe that says \"bulk 4 hours\" might need 6 or 7 in a cold kitchen. If you follow the clock, you'll underproof. Underproofed bread is dense, tight and gummy.\n- Look for the signs instead: dough has grown noticeably (he said around half again in size for his recipe), it's domed, jiggly, has bubbles on the sides and top.\n- If your kitchen is cold: use warmer water when mixing, put the dough in the oven with only the light on, or next to (not on) something warm.\n- Change one thing at a time. Bakers who change everything learn nothing.\n\nHis line: the recipe is a suggestion, the dough is the boss." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "GTD: open loops drain you\n\nNotes from Getting Things Done by David Allen. Read it in 2023, the year I tried to fix my life with a label maker.\n\nCore idea: every unfinished commitment in your head is an \"open loop.\" Call the dentist, reply to Tom, figure out the garage. Each one quietly uses up attention. The mind is great at having ideas and terrible at holding them, so it keeps reminding you at the worst possible times.\n\nHis fix: capture everything outside your head. Get it into a trusted system and decide the very next physical action. Once your brain trusts the system, it stops nagging.\n\nWhat I liked: the feeling after a full brain dump is real. Lighter.\n\nWhat I didn't like: the system turned into a second job. Weekly reviews, contexts, lists of lists. I lasted about five weeks.\n\nMain takeaway I want to keep: unfinished stuff isn't neutral. It costs something just by being unfinished.\n\nLater found a study that made this click (Masicampo and Baumeister, 2011). People with an unfinished goal kept getting distracted by it, as expected. But people who simply wrote down a specific plan for when and how they'd finish it stopped getting distracted, even though the goal was still unfinished. The brain seems to accept \"it's handled\" as closure.\n\nSo it's not finished vs unfinished. It's unfinished with a plan (fine, even useful) vs unfinished without one (noise)." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Julia Child started at 37\n\nJulia Child didn't cook seriously until she was in her late 30s. She moved to Paris with her husband in 1948, had an incredible meal in Rouen (sole meunière), and decided to learn. She enrolled at Le Cordon Bleu in 1949.\n\nShe was bad at first. Tall, clumsy, in a class full of men training to be professional cooks.\n\nShe worked with two French women on a cookbook to teach French cooking to Americans. It took close to a decade. The first publisher rejected it as too long and complicated. Mastering the Art of French Cooking finally came out in 1961, when she was 49. Her TV show started two years later and she became famous after 50.\n\nWhat I love about her book: the recipes explain why. Why the pan has to be hot. Why you dry the meat. Why you don't crowd the mushrooms. She was teaching you to understand, so you could fix things when they went wrong, not just follow steps.\n\nShe also made mistakes on live TV and kept going. Her line was basically: no apologies, nobody knows what it was supposed to look like." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Mise en place\n\nFrench kitchen term, roughly \"everything in its place.\" Before service, every cook chops, measures and sets out everything they'll need, in the order they'll need it. When the orders start flying, they're not thinking, they're reaching.\n\nIt comes from the brigade system Auguste Escoffier organized in the late 1800s. Before that, big kitchens were chaos. He borrowed from the military: clear roles, a chain of command, every station with one job. The chef calls, everyone answers \"Oui, chef.\" Sound familiar? It's a pit crew with knives.\n\nAnthony Bourdain wrote that mise en place was basically his religion. Good cooks keep their station clean while they work because a messy station is a messy head.\n\nWhat I took for home cooking: prep before you turn on the heat. I stopped burning garlic the week I started doing this.\n\nWhat I suspect it's actually about: deciding in advance, when you're calm, so you don't have to decide later, when you're under pressure." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "No time to write this semester\n\nTeaching two sections this semester. My calendar looks like a checkerboard: office hours at 10, meeting at 1, lab meeting at 3:30, a committee thing at 4:30. On paper I have \"free\" hours almost every day. In practice I write nothing.\n\nAn hour between a meeting and a class isn't writing time. By the time I've reread where I left off, it's time to go. So I answer email instead, and feel bad.\n\nWeekends are the only time I get real work done, and I'm getting tired of that.\n\nWhat I can actually change: I set my own office hours, I can move lab meeting to the afternoon if I ask, and I can say no to the committee thing.\n\nWhat I haven't figured out: how to protect long blocks when every individual meeting looks so small on the calendar." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Sourdough attempt #6\n\nAnother brick. Dense crumb, tight, gummy in the middle, barely rose in the oven. Same as attempts 3, 4 and 5.\n\nWhat I did, following the recipe exactly:\n- starter fed the night before, it was bubbly\n- mixed at 9am, stretch and folds every 30 min for 2 hours\n- bulk fermentation 4 hours like the recipe says\n- shaped, overnight in the fridge, baked at 250C in the dutch oven\n\nThe recipe has hundreds of good reviews so I don't think it's the recipe. I'm doing every step on time. I even bought a scale.\n\nPossible culprits: flour? My oven? The starter not strong enough?\n\nKitchen is freezing now that it's fall, I've been baking in a sweater, not sure that matters.\n\nI'm about to buy a new flour and a new starter and change everything at once, which I know is dumb." },
  { domain: "Start here", text: "Start here\n\nThis is a sample vault. It belongs to a made-up person: a fifth-year PhD student who also writes a newsletter and is pitching a first book. They read constantly, highlight everything, save notes from books, papers, podcasts and late night rabbit holes. Then forget almost all of it.\n\nProbably sounds familiar.\n\nHere's the problem nobody talks about. Saving a note feels like keeping it. It isn't. Six months later you're stuck on something at work, and the exact answer is sitting in a note you wrote on a Sunday afternoon about Formula 1. You'll never search for it, because you don't remember it exists. You can't type a search for something you forgot.\n\nThat's the villain of this vault. Not bad notes. Forgotten ones.\n\nIt has beaten smarter people than us. Newton lost his own proof. Fleming's penicillin paper sat on a shelf for ten years while people died of infected scratches. Leonardo filled thousands of pages and kept scribbling a line asking if anything had ever been finished.\n\nYou don't need a better filing system. You need your old notes to show up on their own, at the moment they matter.\n\nThat's what Ocreda does. You write. While you write, it reads what you've saved before and brings back the notes that apply to this moment, quoting your own words so you can see why. Some solve the problem you're on. Some contradict you. Some extend what you're saying, or point at a pattern you didn't see.\n\nHow to try it with this vault:\n1. Open a new note.\n2. Write a few honest sentences about something you're stuck on right now. A chapter that won't start, an argument with a hole in it, a decision you keep going back and forth on. Don't make it pretty.\n3. Watch the side panel.\n\nThe people in these notes are famous. The problem isn't. Every one of them had what they needed somewhere in their own past. Most of them found it by accident.\n\nYou won't have to." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "The drawer\n\nMy grandmother had a kitchen drawer. Rubber bands, a key to nothing, birthday candles, batteries that may or may not work, menus from restaurants that closed years ago. Everything in that drawer was saved on purpose. Nothing in it was ever found on purpose.\n\nMy notes app is that drawer.\n\nI counted once. Around 1,400 notes. I could tell you what maybe 40 of them say.\n\nThe weird part is that saving felt productive every single time. Highlight, clip, add to notes, done. A little hit of having learned something. But learning isn't the save. Learning is the moment you use it, and the moment you need it is never the moment you saved it.\n\nThings I know are in the drawer somewhere:\n- a fix for the dense bread problem\n- something about how a hospital copied a racing team\n- a quote about stopping writing in the middle (Hemingway?)\n- notes from that habits book I swore changed my life\n\nI can't search for them because I don't remember the words I used. I barely remember they exist. Which is the whole problem. Search only works for things you already know you have.\n\nWhat I actually want isn't a better drawer. It's for the drawer to open itself when I'm standing in front of it with the right problem." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "Ulysses tied himself to the mast\n\nIn the Odyssey, Ulysses (Odysseus) has to sail past the Sirens, whose singing makes sailors steer onto the rocks. He wants to hear the song. So he has his crew plug their ears with wax and tie him to the mast, with orders to ignore anything he says until they're past.\n\nHe knew in advance that his future self would make a bad decision. So his calm self made it impossible.\n\nEconomists call this a commitment device. Modern versions:\n- Save More Tomorrow, a program by Richard Thaler and Shlomo Benartzi. Employees agree now to save a portion of their future raises. Doesn't hurt today, and later the money goes to savings before they can miss it. Savings rates went up dramatically.\n- Putting your alarm across the room.\n- Paying for a class in advance so you show up.\n- Deleting the app instead of trying to resist it.\n\nThe point isn't willpower. It's admitting you'll be weak later and setting it up while you're strong." },
  { domain: "Life", text: "You can't search for what you forgot exists\n\nSearch assumes three things. You remember the thing exists. You remember roughly where it is. You remember the words you used.\n\nForgetting breaks the first one, and then the other two don't matter.\n\nEvery tool I've tried is good at the second and third. Folders, tags, links, full text search. They all help you find something you already know you're looking for.\n\nBut the best notes usually come from another part of your life. You wrote about bird beaks and the problem is train noise (this actually happened, the bullet train and the kingfisher). Nobody types \"kingfisher\" while redesigning a train.\n\nSo the question isn't \"how do I find my notes.\" It's \"how do my notes find me.\"\n\nVannevar Bush was basically asking this in 1945. Still mostly unanswered." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Chance favors the prepared mind\n\nLouis Pasteur, in a lecture in 1854. The line usually translated as: in the fields of observation, chance favors only the prepared mind.\n\nHe was talking about discoveries that look like accidents. They're not pure luck. The accident happens to lots of people. It only becomes a discovery for the person who already has the knowledge to recognize what they're seeing.\n\nPlenty of people had probably seen mold kill bacteria on a dish and thrown it out. Fleming had been studying antibacterial substances for years, so he saw it.\n\nLots of people had picked burrs off their dogs. De Mestral was an engineer, so he looked at them under a microscope.\n\nThe flip side: being prepared means having the right knowledge available at the moment of the accident. Not somewhere in a notebook from five years ago. Available, right then.\n\nWhich is the hard part." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Darwin's golden rule\n\nFrom Darwin's autobiography. For years he followed what he called a golden rule: whenever he came across a published fact, an observation or a thought that went against his general results, he wrote a note about it immediately. Without fail.\n\nWhy? Because he'd noticed those facts slipped out of his memory much faster than the ones that agreed with him.\n\nRead that again. Darwin, about as careful a thinker as has ever lived, caught his own memory editing the evidence. Not on purpose. It just quietly kept what agreed with him and let the rest go.\n\nHe said this was why hardly any objection was raised against his views that he hadn't already noticed and tried to answer.\n\nTwo lessons:\n- Your memory is not neutral. It's on your side, which makes it a bad judge.\n- The notes that disagree with you are the most valuable ones you have, and the most likely to be forgotten.\n\nI don't do this. I save quotes that agree with me. My notes are a fan club." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Ebbinghaus and the forgetting curve\n\nHermann Ebbinghaus, Germany, 1880s. He wanted to measure memory scientifically, which nobody had done. So he used himself as the subject. He memorized lists of nonsense syllables (things like \"DAX,\" \"BOK\") so meaning couldn't help, and then tested how much he remembered after different amounts of time.\n\nPublished in 1885. The result is the forgetting curve. You lose most of new information fast, a big chunk within the first day, and then the loss slows down.\n\nThe second finding is the one that matters: each time you review, the curve gets flatter. You forget more slowly. And reviews spaced out over time work better than cramming them together.\n\nSo memory isn't a recording. It leaks unless something brings it back. And the best time to bring something back is right before you'd lose it.\n\nThis is the uncomfortable math behind my 1,400 notes. Without something bringing them back, almost all of them are past the steep part of the curve." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Einstein at the patent office\n\nAt 16, Einstein imagined chasing a beam of light. If you ran alongside it at the same speed, what would you see? A frozen wave? That seemed wrong, and it bothered him for years.\n\nHe couldn't get an academic job after university. In 1902 he started working as a clerk at the patent office in Bern, checking inventions. A lot of them were about electrical devices and keeping clocks in sync between train stations. He did physics on the side, in the evenings, and talked ideas through with a couple of friends.\n\n1905, his \"miracle year.\" Four papers, one of them special relativity. The light beam question from when he was 16 finally had its answer, about ten years later.\n\nTwo years after that, still at the patent office, he had what he called the happiest thought of his life: a person falling from a roof wouldn't feel their own weight. That became the start of general relativity.\n\nWhat I notice: the old question never went away. It sat there for a decade until his daily work (clocks, signals, timing) gave him the pieces. The day job wasn't a distraction from the big idea. It fed it." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Feynman and the wobbling plate\n\nRichard Feynman, after the war, at Cornell. He was burned out. Thought he was finished as a physicist. Every problem felt like a duty.\n\nSo he decided to just play. Do physics only for fun, only things that interested him, no matter how unimportant.\n\nIn the cafeteria someone threw a plate in the air as a joke. Feynman noticed the plate wobbled as it spun and the red Cornell logo on it went around at a different rate than the wobble. He worked out the math for the relationship just because it was fun.\n\nHe showed it to Hans Bethe, who asked what the use was. Feynman said there wasn't one, he was just having fun.\n\nBut working on how things rotate led him back into the physics of how electrons spin. And according to Feynman, the diagrams and the whole body of work he got the Nobel Prize for came from playing around with that plate.\n\nWhen he stopped trying to be important, he did the important work. Play kept him close to the problem without the pressure that had made him freeze." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Feynman's twelve favorite problems\n\nFrom Gian-Carlo Rota, a mathematician who knew him. Rota said Feynman told him how he worked: keep about a dozen of your favorite problems always present in your mind. Most of the time they just sit there. But every time you hear or read a new trick or a new result, you test it against each of your twelve problems to see if it helps.\n\nEvery once in a while, there's a hit. And people say, how did he do it? He must be a genius.\n\nThat's the whole method. Keep your questions alive, and run everything new past them.\n\nWhat I like is that it reverses how most of us take in information. We read something, file it under its own topic, move on. Feynman read something and immediately asked which of his problems it might solve. The new idea didn't go into a folder, it went into a conversation with his open questions.\n\nProblem: holding twelve problems in your head is hard. Holding twelve problems plus everything you've ever read is impossible. Even for Feynman." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Fleming's lucky weather\n\nA detail about the penicillin story that I didn't know. When later researchers tried to recreate Fleming's discovery by putting penicillin mold on a dish with bacteria, it often didn't work. The bacteria grew too fast and the mold never got a chance.\n\nOne reconstruction (by Ronald Hare, who had worked at St Mary's) looked at weather records for London that summer. There was a cool spell while Fleming was away, then it warmed up. The cool temperatures let the slow mold get established first, before the bacteria took off. Then the warm weather let the bacteria grow, everywhere except around the mold.\n\nSo the discovery depended on the temperature of the room. Too warm the whole time and there would have been nothing to see.\n\nTemperature decides which living thing wins and how fast things grow. Fleming wasn't controlling it, the weather was.\n\nAlso a nice reminder that a lot of \"bad luck\" or \"good luck\" in an experiment is just a variable nobody's measuring." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Fleming's mold sat on a shelf for ten years\n\nSeptember 1928. Alexander Fleming comes back from holiday to his famously messy lab at St Mary's in London. One of the dishes of staph bacteria he left out has mold growing on it, and around the mold the bacteria are dead. A clear ring.\n\nHe grows the mold, finds its juice kills a lot of dangerous bacteria, calls it penicillin, and publishes a paper in 1929.\n\nAnd then, not much. It was hard to produce, unstable, and he couldn't purify it. He wasn't a chemist. He used it for a few lab things and moved on. The paper sat in the literature.\n\nFor about ten years, the most important medicine of the century existed in a journal. People kept dying of infected cuts, pneumonia, infections after childbirth. The answer had been written down.\n\nThe popular version gets this wrong. The discovery isn't the hero of the story. The retrieval is. Ten years later, two people in Oxford proved it." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Florey and Chain dug it back out\n\n1938-39, Oxford. Howard Florey (pathologist) and Ernst Chain (biochemist) are surveying natural substances that kill bacteria. Chain goes through old papers and pulls out Fleming's 1929 penicillin paper. Ten years old, mostly ignored.\n\nThey do what Fleming couldn't. Norman Heatley on their team rigs a production setup out of bedpans, milk churns, bathtubs and a bookcase. In May 1940 they test it on infected mice. The treated mice live, the others die.\n\nFebruary 1941. Albert Alexander, an Oxford policeman, has a scratch (from a rose bush, in the usual telling) that has turned into a terrible infection. They give him penicillin. Within days he's dramatically better. Then they run out. They even recover penicillin from his urine to reuse it. It isn't enough. He relapses and dies.\n\nBy D-Day there's enough penicillin for the Allied troops. Fleming, Florey and Chain share the Nobel in 1945.\n\nThe difference between 1929 and 1940 wasn't a new discovery. It was someone pulling an old note into a new moment, with the right people around it. That's it. That's the whole gap." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Gawande's surgical checklist\n\nAtul Gawande, The Checklist Manifesto (2009). He's a surgeon, and he helped the World Health Organization design a 19 item checklist for surgery. Stuff like: confirm the patient and the procedure, everyone in the room says their name and role, check for allergies, check that the antibiotic was given, count the sponges.\n\nSurgeons hated the idea at first. They're experts. Checklists are for beginners.\n\nThey tested it in eight hospitals around the world, rich and poor. Deaths dropped by around half. Major complications dropped by about a third.\n\nThe part I find most interesting: one of the biggest effects came from the introductions. Just going around and saying names made people more likely to speak up when something looked wrong. A nurse who's said her name out loud is more likely to say \"wait.\"\n\nGawande's point is that we fail in two ways: not knowing enough (ignorance) and not using what we know (ineptitude, his word). Modern failure is mostly the second kind. We know. We just don't apply it at the right moment.\n\nI underlined that twice." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Mendel's paper sat for 34 years\n\nGregor Mendel, a monk in Brno, spent about eight years crossing pea plants. Tens of thousands of them. Tall vs short, wrinkled vs smooth, green vs yellow. He counted everything and found ratios, roughly 3 to 1, that kept showing up. In 1865 he presented it to the local natural history society. In 1866 it was printed in their journal.\n\nAlmost nobody noticed. It was cited only a handful of times over the next three decades. Darwin, who badly needed a theory of heredity for his own work and was alive and writing the whole time, never seems to have read it.\n\nMendel became abbot in 1868 and spent his time running the monastery and fighting a tax dispute. He died in 1884.\n\nIn 1900, three botanists working on heredity (de Vries, Correns and Tschermak) each went digging through old literature and each found Mendel. Basically at the same time. Genetics starts that year, 34 years after the paper came out.\n\nWhat gets me: the answer existed. It was printed, it was in libraries. The person who needed it most was working on exactly that problem. Being written down is not the same as being found. The paper didn't need to be better. It needed to meet the right person at the right moment." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Semmelweis told doctors to wash their hands\n\nVienna General Hospital, 1840s. It had two maternity clinics. In the one run by doctors and medical students, far more mothers died of childbed fever than in the one run by midwives. Women begged to be put in the midwives' clinic.\n\nIgnaz Semmelweis, a young doctor, couldn't stop thinking about the difference. Then a colleague cut his finger during an autopsy and died with symptoms that looked like childbed fever. Semmelweis realized the doctors were going straight from dissecting bodies to delivering babies, carrying something on their hands.\n\nIn 1847 he made them wash with a chlorine solution. Deaths in his clinic dropped dramatically, to around the level of the midwives' clinic.\n\nThe medical establishment mostly rejected it. The idea that gentlemen doctors' hands were killing patients was insulting, and he couldn't explain why it worked (germ theory came later, with Pasteur and Lister). He was also bad at persuading people and got angry. He died in an asylum in 1865.\n\nThe answer was there, written down, with numbers. People didn't use it because it contradicted what they believed. Sometimes the problem isn't forgetting the evidence. It's not wanting to remember it." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "The bullet train that copied a bird\n\nJapan, 1990s. The new Shinkansen trains are fast, but there's a problem. When a train shoots into a tunnel at high speed, it pushes a wave of air ahead of it, and when that wave leaves the other end it makes a loud boom. People living near tunnels complained, and noise rules were strict.\n\nEiji Nakatsu, an engineer at JR West, happened to be a serious birdwatcher. He thought about the kingfisher, which dives from the air into water, a much denser medium, and hardly makes a splash. Its long, wedge shaped beak lets it slice through.\n\nSo they modeled the nose of the train on a kingfisher's beak. The 500 Series came out in 1997 with a long pointed nose. The tunnel boom problem was solved, and the train used less electricity and ran faster.\n\nHe also used owl feathers (owls fly almost silently) to quiet the part on the roof that connects to the power lines, and a penguin's body shape for another part.\n\nThe answer was in his hobby. Not in engineering papers. You'd never find it by searching \"train noise.\"" },
  { domain: "Science", text: "The waiter who remembered every unpaid order\n\nBerlin, 1920s. The psychologist Kurt Lewin is in a café with students and notices something about the waiter. He remembers every detail of the orders that haven't been paid yet. The moment a bill is settled, it's gone. He can't recall it at all.\n\nHis student Bluma Zeigarnik turned that into experiments in 1927. People were given simple tasks and interrupted in some of them. Afterward they remembered the interrupted tasks noticeably better than the finished ones.\n\nThat's the Zeigarnik effect. Unfinished things hold on. Your brain keeps them open, like a tab you can't close.\n\nIt explains a lot:\n- why cliffhangers work\n- why a half-done chore nags at you at 2am\n- why stopping mid-sentence makes you want to come back (Hemingway figured this out without the science)\n\nThe finished task is a paid bill. Your mind lets it go." },
  { domain: "Science", text: "Velcro came from a dog walk\n\n1941, the Swiss Alps. George de Mestral, an engineer, comes back from a hike with his dog and both of them are covered in burrs from burdock plants. Annoying. Most people would just pick them off.\n\nHe put one under a microscope instead. The burr was covered in tiny hooks that caught on anything with a loop: fur, the threads of his pants.\n\nHooks on one side, loops on the other. It took him years to figure out how to make it from nylon, and he patented it in the 1950s. He called it Velcro, from the French words for velvet and hook.\n\nNobody took it seriously at first, it looked cheap. Then NASA used it to keep things from floating around in space, and suddenly it was everywhere.\n\nWhat I like: the irritating thing was the answer. The burrs were the problem on the walk and the solution in the workshop. Same object, different question." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Agatha Christie's 73 notebooks\n\nAgatha Christie kept something like 73 notebooks over her career. Not neat. No system. She'd grab whichever was closest. Plot ideas next to shopping lists, possible murder methods next to notes about the garden. Sometimes she started from the back, or the middle.\n\nA researcher, John Curran, went through all of them and found something great. Ideas jotted in one decade often show up in a book written ten or twenty years later. A poison, a trick with a will, a type of character. She'd flip through, find something she'd forgotten writing, and it became the center of a new book.\n\nSo the mess wasn't the problem. The messy notebooks were her idea bank for fifty years. But she had to physically flip through them. A good idea only came back if she happened to open the right notebook on the right day.\n\nHow many plots died in notebook 41 because she never reopened it?\n\n(same thing with my chapter ideas, honestly)" },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Am I missing the paper that matters?\n\nLit review panic. My committee meeting is in five weeks, and the thing I'm scared of is someone saying \"how did you miss X?\" and X being the paper that changes everything.\n\nI've searched the databases every way I can think of. But search only finds what I already know to ask for. The paper that would really change my argument probably uses different words, comes from a different field, or is old enough that nobody cites it anymore.\n\nAlso, half my own reading notes from year one I don't remember. I know there are highlights in there from a book on organizational change, a whole folder on a method I dropped, things I clipped from blogs at 1am. Some of it is probably exactly relevant and I'd never think to look.\n\nIt's a weird feeling. The thing I'm afraid of missing might already be in my own notes." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Chapter 3 won't start\n\nFifth year. Chapters 1 and 2 are drafted. Chapter 3 is the one that holds the whole dissertation together and I've been \"about to start\" it since November.\n\nEvery morning goes the same. I open the file, reread what I wrote last time, fix commas for an hour, tell myself I'll start the real section after lunch. After lunch I answer emails. At night I lie in bed thinking about it, which is worse than writing it.\n\nI don't think it's a knowledge problem. I've read everything. I have something like 300 notes on the sources for this chapter. It's that every session starts cold, from zero, and the blank part of the page is too big.\n\nIt also sits on me all week. Even on days I'm not supposed to be writing, it's there in the back of my head, like an unpaid bill.\n\nMy advisor wants a full draft by May. What I need is a way to start each morning already in motion, and a way to stop carrying it around 24 hours a day." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Conference talk in three weeks\n\n20 minute talk at the big conference in my field. First time on a main stage, not a poster.\n\nI have 47 slides. That's too many, I know. Each one has bullet points, and when I practice I can tell I'm skipping the connections. Slide 12 says one thing, slide 13 says another, and I'm hoping the audience connects them because honestly I'm not sure I can explain how they connect.\n\nMy labmate watched me practice and said: \"I understand every slide and I don't know what your argument is.\" Brutal. Fair.\n\nThe worst part is I do know my argument when I talk about it over coffee. Something about the slides breaks it into pieces.\n\nI need to figure out what the one thing is, and build everything else around it. Three weeks." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Emily Dickinson's drawer of poems\n\nWhen Emily Dickinson died in 1886, her sister Lavinia found close to 1,800 poems among her things. Many were sewn into little handmade booklets, others written on scraps, envelope flaps, the backs of recipes.\n\nFewer than a dozen were published while she was alive, mostly without her name and edited to sound like what editors thought poems should sound like.\n\nLavinia had promised to burn Emily's letters, and she did. But she didn't burn the poems. She pushed to get them published. The first collection came out in 1890.\n\nSo one of the most important poets in American history was, while she lived, a woman with a drawer full of writing nobody read. The work existed. It just had nowhere to go.\n\nWhat saved it wasn't more writing. It was one person who opened the drawer and decided it mattered." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Hemingway stopped in the middle\n\nHemingway's trick, which he repeated in a few places (an Esquire piece in 1935, and later in A Moveable Feast): always stop for the day while it's going well and you know what happens next. Don't write yourself empty.\n\nHis logic was that if you stop when you're stuck, tomorrow starts with being stuck. If you stop in the middle of a scene you're excited about, tomorrow starts with momentum. You sit down and you already know the next sentence.\n\nHe also said not to think about the story between sessions. Let the back of your mind work on it.\n\nRoald Dahl did something similar. He said he never wrote himself out, he'd stop when he knew where it was going so he'd be eager to get back.\n\nThe counterintuitive part is that it feels wasteful. You're on a roll, why stop? Because the roll is what you're saving for tomorrow. You're leaving yourself a lit match instead of a cold stove.\n\nTried it twice in 2024. Worked both times, then I forgot about it." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "J.K. Rowling and the delayed train\n\n1990. J.K. Rowling is on a train from Manchester to London and it's delayed for about four hours. Out of nowhere, the idea of a boy who doesn't know he's a wizard walks into her head, almost fully formed. She didn't have a working pen and was too shy to ask anyone for one, so she just thought about it for the whole trip.\n\nThat evening she started writing. Then came five years of building the world before the first book was finished. Her notes were all over the place: scraps of paper, notebooks, napkins from cafés. She kept boxes of them. She worked out the plots of all seven books in advance, and one of her handwritten planning grids, the one for Order of the Phoenix, became famous. Columns for each subplot, rows for each chapter.\n\nThings planted in book one paid off in book seven, because she'd written them down years before.\n\nThe first book was rejected by around twelve publishers. Bloomsbury took it in 1996, apparently after the chairman's eight year old daughter read the first chapter and demanded more.\n\nThe idea took four hours. Making it work took years of notes that had to come back at the right time." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "My advisor says I ignore the counterevidence\n\nMeeting with my advisor today. Her main comment on the chapter 2 draft: \"Your argument is clean, too clean. Where are the cases that don't fit?\"\n\nShe's right and I hate it. I know there were studies that cut against my main claim. I remember reading at least two. I didn't put them in. Not on purpose, exactly. When I sat down to write I just didn't think of them.\n\nLooking at my notes now, almost everything I saved supports my position. I highlight what agrees with me. The stuff that bugged me, I read, felt uncomfortable, and moved on without writing anything down.\n\nIf I don't fix this, the defense will. Someone on the committee will bring up exactly the thing I left out.\n\nI need to go back and find the evidence against me, and I need a habit so that this doesn't happen again in chapters 3 and 4." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Newsletter: nothing to write about this week\n\nIssue #41 is due Thursday and I have nothing. Which is ridiculous, because I read about 20 things a week and save half of them.\n\nThe problem: when I sit down to pick a topic, my mind goes blank. I scroll through my saved articles and nothing jumps out, because I'm reading them cold, one by one, with no question in mind. Everything feels either too small or already said by someone else.\n\nThe issues people liked most were never planned. Issue #17 (the one that got shared everywhere) came from me noticing that something I'd read about restaurants explained something about my research. Two things from different parts of my life that turned out to be the same thing. I can't make that happen on purpose.\n\nWhat I want: a way to stop starting from a blank list every week. Some kind of running set of questions I care about, so that when I read something new I already know what it might connect to." },
  { domain: "Writing", text: "Reviewer 2 destroyed the paper\n\nReviews came back on the first paper from my dissertation. Reviewer 1: minor revisions, nice. Reviewer 2: four pages. Calls the framing \"naive,\" says the main finding is \"unsurprising,\" lists eleven problems.\n\nI spent a day being angry. Then a day being sure I'm bad at this. Today I read it again, and about seven of the eleven points are actually right. Annoyingly right.\n\nWhat bugs me is that I could have caught most of this myself before submitting, if anyone had read the draft honestly. My labmates said \"looks great.\" My advisor was traveling. The only person who told me the truth was an anonymous stranger, after it was too late.\n\nFor chapter 3 and the book I want a better way to get real feedback earlier, from people who'll actually be honest. Without it turning into everyone rewriting my stuff the way they'd do it." },
];
